NOW asynchronous native endpoint prototype
==========================================

Compatibility and opt-in
------------------------

NowProtoClientContext remains exactly { handle, custom, Write, Read }. Existing
consumers receive that context through drdynvc's OnChannelConnected and
OnChannelDisconnected. Without direct opt-in, NOW rejects a second simultaneous
offer and uses legacy Read/Write. A disconnected legacy Write returns
ERROR_NOT_CONNECTED. The context remains session-owned.

Legacy Write retains its historical synchronous/raw-channel semantics, not the
new endpoint lifetime guarantees. Existing consumers must still coordinate raw
legacy operations with channel/session lifetime. In particular this prototype
does not make legacy Write versus remote close/teardown safe by serialization.
Use the direct interface for supervised asynchronous operations. NativeSessions
and RDM bindings are not included or changed.

freerdp_nowproto_client_set_callbacks(context, callbacks) explicitly opts into
NowProtoClientCallbacksV2 from <freerdp/client/nowproto.h>. Set size to sizeof the
struct and version to NOWPROTO_CLIENT_CALLBACKS_VERSION_2. All four callbacks
are required. The table is copied and frozen. Install it before the first
OnOpen; the first NOW OnChannelConnected is an appropriate attachment point.
After opt-in legacy Write returns ERROR_INVALID_STATE; legacy Read is not used.

This revision changes the experimental interop contract from the initial
retained-channel prototype: Write/Close become asynchronous submissions with
explicit operation completion. The legacy four-pointer ABI is unchanged.

V2 ABI (native C layout, including size_t and pointer alignment):

  NowProtoClientCallbacksV2:
    UINT32 size, version;
    void* custom;
    void Offer(void* custom, NowProtoEndpoint* endpoint);
    void Receive(void* custom, NowProtoEndpoint* endpoint,
                 const BYTE* data, size_t size);
    void Closed(void* custom, NowProtoEndpoint* endpoint);
    void OperationCompleted(void* custom, NowProtoEndpoint* endpoint,
                            void* token, UINT status);

  NowProtoEndpoint (function pointers, endpoint first):
    void Retain(endpoint);
    void Release(endpoint);
    UINT SubmitWrite(endpoint, const BYTE* data, UINT32 size,
                     void* token, BOOL* queued);
    UINT RequestClose(endpoint, void* token, BOOL* queued);

Direct mode requires the default asynchronous DVC worker. Synchronous DVC mode
and transport replay return ERROR_NOT_SUPPORTED on opt-in; legacy mode remains
available. No session-wide SynchronousDynamicChannels setting is changed.

Offer, submission and callback contract
---------------------------------------

Every offer has an independent refcounted endpoint. Direct mode accepts a
replacement while its predecessor is still open; native code does not choose
a winner or retire the predecessor. Offer runs on OnOpen after the CREATE
response. Receive and Closed always carry the same endpoint. Closed occurs
once per offered endpoint, including remote/local close and teardown.
The listener-level drdynvc interface deliberately stays the legacy context.

Endpoints and receive buffers are borrowed during callbacks. Retain the endpoint
and copy receive bytes before handing them to asynchronous work. Release every
owned reference, even after Closed or session teardown. Each operation requires
an owned reference. Racing release of the final reference with a new operation
is a consumer error. Internal callback/command references permit releasing a
consumer reference from a callback, including Closed during a local close.

SubmitWrite copies nonempty data before publishing a command. Null/empty data
is rejected with ERROR_INVALID_PARAMETER. queued must be nonnull. A token is an
opaque correlation value; native code neither dereferences nor owns it.

  Return success, queued=TRUE:
    Exactly one OperationCompleted, with the same endpoint/token.
  Return success, queued=FALSE:
    RequestClose on an already-closed endpoint; no completion callback.
  Return failure, queued=FALSE:
    Submission rejected; no completion callback.

Completion can run on another thread before the submission returns. Establish
token ownership before calling; do not infer callback ordering from the return.
Rejected or no-op submissions never call back, including after session teardown.
Writes after Closed return ERROR_NOT_CONNECTED. Close is idempotent and always
targets its original offer, even after a wire ID is reused.

OperationCompleted reports the native operation's return value, or
ERROR_OPERATION_ABORTED for accepted work canceled before execution at shutdown.
It is not a wire-delivery or remote acknowledgement. Base drdynvc still maps
some disconnected-send outcomes to success and can overwrite close-PDU send
failure with callback success. This revision does not strengthen those results.
An accepted write can complete ERROR_NOT_CONNECTED if remote close wins.
Closed can precede OperationCompleted for a local close or a stale command.

All direct callbacks run on the drdynvc worker, and must only copy/enqueue
nonblocking work. Reentrant submissions enqueue; they never execute inline,
even from Offer/Receive/Closed/completion. Never wait for managed async work,
join a submitting thread from a callback, pump the worker recursively, or
destroy the session inline. No NOW plugin lock is held during user callbacks;
core receive/close callbacks can hold the native channel-table lock.
Root delegates, callback code, and custom state until native teardown returns.
No accepted command callback can survive worker join/plugin termination.

Dispatcher and transport lifetime
----------------------------------

The NOW plugin acquires an asynchronous dispatcher during Initialize, even when
used through the legacy ABI. Other DVC plugins need not opt in. The exported
core seam in <freerdp/client/drdynvc.h> consists of dispatcher
acquire/retain/release/post. Acquire is restricted to plugin initialization with
a borrowed live manager; subsequent operations use a retained dispatcher, never
a manager pointer. No public IWTS* structure layout changes.

A dispatcher is a terminal connection epoch, not a retained native channel or
channel table. STARTING rejects tasks with ERROR_NOT_READY. The worker publishes
RUNNING on entry. A short lock fences task publication and incoming PDU enqueue
against STOPPING. The worker claims permission to start an operation under that
same lock, then releases it before invoking code. An already-claimed operation
may finish after shutdown starts; unstarted accepted tasks cancel explicitly.
Success transfers task payload ownership; rejection does not.

Shutdown closes admission and signals a preallocated stop event (no allocating
QUIT message). The worker drains remaining commands with aborted completions
outside queue/dispatcher locks, releases PDU streams, tombstones the epoch, and
closes native channels. The connection owner joins before plugin termination or
queue destruction. Startup failure accepts no tasks. Unexpected worker wait or
dequeue failure also fences admission before cancellation. Failed stop wake/join
reports an error and retains live resources instead of freeing a running worker.
Direct Terminated also performs stop/join if Disconnected was omitted.

Retained dispatchers/endpoints survive teardown inertly. Queue pointers are
detached before destruction. Reconnect creates a fresh epoch, even if WinPR
clears/reopens the same queue; old capabilities can never target it. Disconnect
also clears partial-PDU assembly flags after releasing its stream, so a new
connection does not inherit the predecessor's incomplete message.

Only the worker reads a direct endpoint's raw channel. OnClose tombstones it
before native free. Tasks retain the endpoint, not a channel ID. Consequently
remote close, local write/close, and retirement share one owner. Offer-submitted
Close runs only after OnOpen's native caller has returned.

The initial retained-channel lease, per-channel handle fields, retained table
lock, OnOpen pin, table-clear gate and special close-error plumbing are removed.
Channel creation, Close, refcount, OnOpen and table ownership are back to base
semantics. No generic_dynvc listener behavior changes. No negotiation, winner
publication, deadlines, or queue policy is added; producers must bound their
outstanding work. This is not a hard-real-time dispatcher: posting allocates and
takes short native locks.

Lifecycle calls/network ingress still require FreeRDP's connection-owner
serialization. Arbitrary concurrent session destruction, late callbacks after
the transport has been destroyed, module unload with retained function pointers,
blocking/throwing task callbacks and asynchronous thread termination are not
supported. A failed OS wake/join requires a successful retry before resources
can be reclaimed; cancellation cannot guarantee progress if its worker cannot
run. Native task callbacks must return normally.

Validation
-----------

Use BUILD_TESTING=OFF, BUILD_TESTING_INTERNAL=ON, WITH_CLIENT_COMMON=ON and
CHANNEL_NOWPROTO_CLIENT=ON. Targets: TestNowProto and TestDrdynvc.

TestNowProtoLifetime drives the real async worker through an in-process
static-channel PDU transport: routing, overlapping offers, reused wire IDs,
deferred Close from Offer/Receive, callback reference release, copied write
buffers, transport errors, reentrant completion, concurrent producers versus
local/remote close, exactly-once shutdown cancellation, reentrant teardown,
retained endpoints/epochs across teardown/reconnect, direct Terminated, and
legacy/synchronous-mode compatibility. It does not merely serialize managed
API calls or force SynchronousDynamicChannels.

TestNowProtoDispatchFaults compiles production sources with translation-unit
local shims and renamed public symbols, not production fault hooks. It tests
dispatcher/endpoint/command allocation failure, enqueue failure and stream/ref
ownership, pre-start rejection, worker creation failure, partial startup teardown,
unexpected wait/dequeue failure (including removal before error), and wake/join
failure without freeing live resources. Accepted tasks complete/cancel exactly
once; rejected tasks never complete.

macOS arm64 commands/results for this revision:

  cmake --build build/now-native --target TestDrdynvc TestNowProto --parallel 4
  ctest --test-dir build/now-native \
    -R 'Test(NowProto(Lifetime|DispatchFaults)|DrdynvcUnknownChannel)$' \
    --repeat until-fail:50 --output-on-failure --timeout 60

Passed: all three tests, 50 repetitions each. The Debug/minimal-library build
uses ENABLE_WARNING_VERBOSE=OFF and CHANNEL_RDPECAM_CLIENT=OFF (no libv4l).

  cmake --build build/now-native-asan --target TestDrdynvc TestNowProto --parallel 4
  ASAN_OPTIONS=detect_leaks=0 UBSAN_OPTIONS=halt_on_error=1 \
    ctest --test-dir build/now-native-asan \
      -R 'Test(NowProto(Lifetime|DispatchFaults)|DrdynvcUnknownChannel)$' \
      --repeat until-fail:20 --output-on-failure --timeout 60

Passed: all three tests, 20 repetitions each with address/undefined sanitizers.
LeakSanitizer is disabled on macOS. WITH_UNICODE_BUILTIN=OFF avoids the previously
reproduced built-in Unicode UB in the unrelated regression test.

These are native in-process tests, not live-server interoperability or RDM
supervision parity. OS failures are injected, not claims about recoverability
after arbitrary handle corruption. Windows/Linux runtime validation remains
outstanding.

  cmake --build build/now-native-tsan --target TestDrdynvc TestNowProto --parallel 4
  TSAN_OPTIONS=halt_on_error=1 \
    ctest --test-dir build/now-native-tsan \
      -R 'Test(NowProto(Lifetime|DispatchFaults)|DrdynvcUnknownChannel)$' \
      --output-on-failure --timeout 60

ThreadSanitizer is NOT clean: the final run stops all three tests at the existing
freerdp_timer_free/timer_thread race. An earlier run of this revision reached
critical.c:193 EnterCriticalSection lock metadata in the async lifetime test.
These reproduce the previously observed timer/WinPR limitations. No suppressions
or unrelated runtime fixes are included; complete NOW race detection remains
blocked by them.
Logs are retained as revision-build.log/revision-validation.log in build dirs.
